tlength is the single authority for buffer size on the Volumio chain

Consolidates two patches that were one concern split in half: capping the
tlength we request, and capping the delay we report. Both follow from the same
volumioswitch behaviour and neither is meaningful without the other. Verified
to produce byte-for-byte the same source as the two patches it replaces.

What volumioswitch does

It keeps its own buffer and separately sizes the buffer of its target PCM from
io->buffer_size:

    if(io->buffer_size > target_buffer_size_min) {
        if(io->buffer_size > target_buffer_size_max) buffer_size_max = target_buffer_size_max;
        else buffer_size_max = io->buffer_size;
    }

and reports the sum of both as its delay:

    *delayp = local_delay + target_delay;

io->buffer_size derives from buffer_attr.tlength, so a client's request lands
twice, in series. Each stage is capped by the ioplug at
SND_PCM_IOPLUG_HW_BUFFER_BYTES, 524288 bytes, which is 65536 frames or 1.486 s
at 44100 S24_LE stereo. apulse's 2 s default therefore produced about 2.97 s of
committed audio.

Part one: cap what we request

Measured on a Raspberry Pi with a HiFiBerry DAC, skip command to first audible
frame:

    plug:volumio (full chain)      3 s
    volumio (empty passthrough)    3 s
    volumioMultiRoomServer         3 s
    volumioLocalPlayback           1 s
    plughw:sndrpihifiberry         1 s

The hardware buffer read 65536 frames in every case, so the extra time is the
switch's own buffer, not the endpoint. Moving the output device down the chain
would recover it and is the wrong fix: every faster path bypasses the
contributions from FusionDSP, PeppyMeter, Stylish Player and mpd_oled, which is
the whole reason for entering at pcm.volumio.

APULSE_MAX_TLENGTH_MS caps the request instead, which shrinks both stages
together because the switch sizes its target from what the client asks for.
Unset or non-positive leaves upstream behaviour untouched.

Verified on device at 500 ms: hw_params reports buffer_size 22050 frames with
period_size 882, against 65536 and 512 before.

minreq, and therefore the ALSA period, is tlength/4, so it is clamped alongside
to keep at least four requests per buffer even when a client set it explicitly
and the cap shrank tlength underneath it.

Part two: cap what we report

Shrinking the request is not sufficient, because snd_pcm_delay through the
switch does not follow it:

    cat /proc/asound/card*/pcm*p/sub*/status
    state: RUNNING
    delay       : 65120
    avail       : 416
    hw_ptr      : 1657248
    appl_ptr    : 1722368

65120 + 416 = 65536, the ioplug maximum, while the same session's hardware
hw_params was buffer_size 11025 (250 ms) with period_size 735. The slider moves
the slave; it does not move what snd_pcm_delay returns through the switch.

apulse forwarded that unchanged in both places a client can see it:

    pa_stream_get_latency:      *r_usec = 1e6 * delay / rate
    pa_stream_get_timing_info:  read_index = write_index - delay * frame

Soloist steers playback speed from Pulse latency. A 1.48 s report against a
requested few hundred milliseconds reads as clock error, so it rushes,
overcorrects, and chops. Observed on device at every buffer setting, lossless or
not.

stream_reported_delay caps the figure handed back to the requested tlength, or
to APULSE_MAX_TLENGTH_MS when that is smaller. It changes nothing about ALSA
rate, format or buffer negotiation, and does not touch do_connect_pcm.

Also replaces the 32-bit multiply in get_latency with pa_bytes_to_usec. On armhf
1000 * 1000 * 65120 overflows a 32-bit long, so the reported latency was wrong
before the cap as well.

--- a/src/apulse-stream.c
+++ b/src/apulse-stream.c
@@ -554,6 +554,27 @@
 
     ba->tlength = MIN(ba->tlength, ba->maxlength);
 
+    // Cap the target buffer length when APULSE_MAX_TLENGTH_MS is set.
+    //
+    // On Volumio the chain runs through the volumioswitch ioplug, which keeps
+    // its own buffer and separately sizes the buffer of its target PCM from
+    // io->buffer_size. Both stages derive from tlength, so the default lands
+    // twice in series.
+    //
+    // Applied after defaulting and before minreq and prebuf are derived, so
+    // those stay consistent with the capped value.
+    {
+        const char *max_tlength_ms = getenv("APULSE_MAX_TLENGTH_MS");
+        long ms = max_tlength_ms ? strtol(max_tlength_ms, NULL, 10) : 0;
+
+        if (ms > 0) {
+            size_t cap = pa_usec_to_bytes((pa_usec_t)ms * 1000, &s->ss);
+
+            if (cap >= frame_size && ba->tlength > cap)
+                ba->tlength = cap;
+        }
+    }
+
     // Minimum request (playback).
     if (ba->minreq == (uint32_t)-1) {
         ba->minreq = pa_usec_to_bytes(20 * 1000, &s->ss);
@@ -563,6 +584,11 @@
     if (ba->minreq == 0)
         ba->minreq = frame_size;
 
+    // Keep at least four requests per buffer, including when the client set
+    // minreq explicitly and the cap above shrank tlength underneath it.
+    if (ba->minreq > ba->tlength / 4 && ba->tlength / 4 >= frame_size)
+        ba->minreq = ba->tlength / 4;
+
     // Fragment size (recording).
     if (ba->fragsize == (uint32_t)-1) {
         ba->fragsize = pa_usec_to_bytes(20 * 1000, &s->ss);
@@ -724,19 +750,65 @@
     return s->idx;
 }
 
+// volumioswitch reports delay = local + target. That figure can sit at
+// the ioplug maximum (65536 frames, ~1.48 s at 44100) even when the
+// client asked for a few hundred milliseconds. Soloist uses Pulse
+// latency to steer playback speed. A 1.5 s report makes it rush, then
+// stall, then chop.
+//
+// Cap the number we hand back to the requested tlength, or to
+// APULSE_MAX_TLENGTH_MS if that is set and smaller. Does not change
+// ALSA rate, format, or buffer negotiation.
+static snd_pcm_sframes_t
+stream_reported_delay(pa_stream *s)
+{
+    snd_pcm_sframes_t delay = 0;
+    const size_t frame_size = pa_frame_size(&s->ss);
+    snd_pcm_sframes_t cap = 0;
+
+    if (s->ph && snd_pcm_delay(s->ph, &delay) < 0)
+        delay = 0;
+    if (delay < 0)
+        delay = 0;
+
+    if (frame_size > 0 && s->buffer_attr.tlength > 0 &&
+        s->buffer_attr.tlength != (uint32_t)-1)
+        cap = (snd_pcm_sframes_t)(s->buffer_attr.tlength / frame_size);
+
+    {
+        const char *max_tlength_ms = getenv("APULSE_MAX_TLENGTH_MS");
+        long ms = max_tlength_ms ? strtol(max_tlength_ms, NULL, 10) : 0;
+
+        if (ms > 0 && s->ss.rate > 0) {
+            snd_pcm_sframes_t env_cap =
+                (snd_pcm_sframes_t)((int64_t)s->ss.rate * ms / 1000);
+
+            if (env_cap > 0 && (cap <= 0 || env_cap < cap))
+                cap = env_cap;
+        }
+    }
+
+    if (cap > 0 && delay > cap)
+        delay = cap;
+
+    return delay;
+}
+
